{"reference":"Matthew 18","book":"Matthew","chapter":18,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Matthew 18 holds together several concerns that can seem separate at first: greatness is reversed through humility, “little ones” are protected, wrongdoing is addressed with a view to restoration, communal discernment and prayer are named, and forgiveness is pressed to its fullest extent. Reading the chapter as a whole helps keep these themes connected rather than treating the procedures in verses 15–20 or the parable in verses 23–35 as isolated instructions.","verse_refs":["Matthew 18:1-35","Matthew 18:18","Matthew 18:23-35"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_matthew_018_79b5a6433d8f665297a39a1d"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Interpretive Questions","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The language of binding and loosing in verse 18 belongs to a distinctive pattern in Matthew that has generated major disagreement about communal authority. The chapter itself places this saying amid efforts to address sin, hear witnesses, and involve the church, so readers should attend closely to that immediate setting when considering what the saying means.","verse_refs":["Matthew 18:18"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_matthew_018_79b5a6433d8f665297a39a1d"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"Social Setting","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"The king, servants, debts, imprisonment, and mercy in the final parable resonate with an unequal agrarian Roman-Palestinian economy. That setting makes the story’s financial and social relationships part of its force: debt is not merely an abstract figure but appears within relationships marked by unequal power.","verse_refs":["Matthew 18:23-35"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_matthew_018_79b5a6433d8f665297a39a1d"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_4","text":"The severe warning about causing “little ones” to stumble must not be used to excuse abuse, stigmatize disability or mental illness, enforce misogyny or anti-LGBTQ harm, blame poverty, set apocalyptic dates, or map ancient judgments onto modern peoples.","verse_refs":["Matthew 18:6"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_matthew_018_0563ab0e7bc8f8baaef60404"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}